# Copy to deploy/.env.deploy and fill in. That file is gitignored.
#
# YOU MAY ONLY NEED THE FIRST VALUE.
#
# deploy/cloudrun.sh falls back to the repository-root `.env` for the three
# credential-shaped values, mapping `PV_DB_KERNEL` to `COCKROACH_KERNEL_URL` and
# accepting `GEMINI_API_KEY` for `GOOGLE_API_KEY`. If `.env` is already
# configured for local runs — and it is, on the machine this was built on — then
# the only thing this file has to supply is PV_GCP_PROJECT.
#
# That fallback exists so a live credential never has to be copied from one file
# into another. A copy goes via a clipboard, and this project has already paid
# once for a credential that travelled through a channel it should not have.
#
# VALUES ONLY EVER LIVE IN .env, HERE, AND IN SECRET MANAGER. cloudrun.sh writes
# them into Secret Manager and mounts them into the revision by reference.
# Nothing below reaches a command line, a revision spec, or the output of
# `gcloud run services describe`.

# --- required -----------------------------------------------------------------

# The ONLY value with no fallback. Deliberately not read from .env: it is not a
# secret, it decides which account gets billed, and inheriting it silently from
# a file nobody re-reads is how a deploy lands in the wrong project.

# The project ID, not the project number and not the display name.
#   gcloud projects list --format='value(projectId)'
PV_GCP_PROJECT=

# CockroachDB DSNs. Both must resolve to the SAME database — a seed that split
# itself across two databases once reported "26 tables checked, 26 match" over a
# database holding zero evidence rows.
#
# sslmode=verify-full, not require: CockroachDB Cloud presents a real
# certificate and `require` accepts any certificate at all, which is a
# man-in-the-middle away from being no TLS.
COCKROACH_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:26257/provenance?sslmode=verify-full
COCKROACH_KERNEL_URL=postgresql://pv_kernel_writer:PASSWORD@HOST:26257/provenance?sslmode=verify-full

# Google AI Studio key. The Gemini Developer API, which the hackathon rules name
# explicitly. No service account, no ADC, no IAM.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# --- optional -----------------------------------------------------------------

# us-east4 is the same physical metro as the CockroachDB cluster on AWS
# us-east-1, so the cross-cloud hop stays single-digit milliseconds instead of
# 70+. CANONICAL_DECISIONS.md -> Gemini model id canon -> Database.
PV_GCP_REGION=us-east4

# Artifact Registry repository name.
PV_GCP_REPO=provenance

# --- what is NOT here ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The four signing keys — capability HMAC, cursor HMAC, ingest-alias HMAC and
# the local auth secret — are minted by the deploy script on first run and
# stored in Secret Manager. They are deliberately not settable here: a signing
# key pasted into a file gets committed eventually, and re-running `up` must not
# rotate them. Rotating them invalidates every capability proof and pagination
# cursor the previous revision issued, which presents as intermittent 403s
# rather than as a rotation.
#
# To rotate one deliberately:
#   gcloud secrets versions add provenance-capability-hmac-key --data-file=-

# --- the CockroachDB cluster CA certificate ------------------------------------
#
# Optional. Left unset, the deploy script looks in the two places `ccloud` puts
# it: %APPDATA%/postgresql/root.crt on Windows, ~/.postgresql/root.crt elsewhere.
# Set this only if yours is somewhere else.
#
# It is REQUIRED to exist somewhere, and the failure without it is misleading
# twice over. CockroachDB Cloud presents a cluster-specific CA, not a public
# one, so a container that has no copy fails with "root certificate file ...
# does not exist" -- which reads as a bad path. Switching to
# `sslrootcert=system` then fails one step later with "certificate verify
# failed", which reads as though the fix did not take. The certificate has to
# ship; the script mounts it from Secret Manager at /etc/ssl/cockroach/root.crt.
#
# Do NOT "fix" this by downgrading to sslmode=require. `require` accepts any
# certificate at all, so it turns a verified channel into an unverified one and
# the only visible difference is that the error stops.
# PV_CA_CERT_FILE=/path/to/root.crt
